;; Kanata config for macOS with a German (QWERTZ) keyboard layout.
;;
;; Behaviour:
;;   - Every key behaves exactly as on a normal German keyboard by default
;;     (kanata only intercepts the keys listed in defsrc below; all other
;;     keys are passed straight through thanks to process-unmapped-keys).
;;   - Tapping the ö key types "ö" as usual; holding it types "\" (backslash).
;;   - Tapping the ä key types "ä" as usual; holding it types "|" (pipe).
;;   - Tapping the ü key types "ü" as usual; holding it types "~" (tilde).
;;   - Caps Lock is remapped to a "Hyper" key: holding it holds Ctrl+Shift+
;;     Alt+Cmd all at once (for custom app/window-manager shortcuts). It no
;;     longer toggles caps lock. Tapping it alone does nothing by itself,
;;     same as tapping a lone modifier key normally does nothing.
;;
;; Notes on how this works:
;;   - Kanata's key names refer to physical key positions (based on the
;;     standard US/ANSI naming), not to the character your OS layout
;;     produces. On a German keyboard, the physical key in the ";" position
;;     (right of L) is the ö key, the physical key in the "'" position
;;     (right of ö) is the ä key, and the physical key in the "[" position
;;     (right of P) is the ü key - that's why they're named ";", "'", and
;;     "[" in defsrc below.
;;   - Tapping ö/ä/ü just forwards that physical key, so macOS's German
;;     layout still turns it into "ö"/"ä"/"ü" as normal.
;;   - Holding ö/ä/ü outputs "\"/"|"/"~" via kanata's unicode action, which
;;     sends the character directly and does not depend on any key combo
;;     existing for backslash/pipe/tilde in the German layout.
;;   - Hyper is built with kanata's "multi" action, which holds all listed
;;     modifiers together for as long as the physical key is held.
;;   - Holding the "^" key (kanata name: nubs - it reports as KEY_102ND, the
;;     ISO "extra" key, NOT KEY_GRAVE/"grv" despite sitting where a US
;;     grave/backtick key would be - confirmed via `kanata --debug`, don't
;;     assume "grv" without checking) activates the "apps" layer:
;;     the physical key printed "Z" on your German keyboard, plus k/b/c/t,
;;     launch or switch to Zen/kitty/Bitwarden/Claude/Teams via `open -a`,
;;     using kanata's "cmd" action. The same layer also has s/d/o/m, which
;;     go to (or launch) dedicated kitty instances (see below), and the
;;     F1-F12 row, which sends the literal function keys (see the F-row
;;     block below for why that matters). Releasing "^" returns to the
;;     default layer.
;;     "^" was chosen over an earlier fn-based version of this config
;;     because fn sits far from the home row on a full-size external
;;     keyboard, and over an even earlier "a"-based version because "a" is
;;     typed constantly and the tap/hold ambiguity made normal typing feel
;;     laggy. "^" is a German dead key that's rarely pressed in practice, so
;;     it makes a good dual-purpose key: tapping it still composes accents
;;     as normal (e.g. "^" then "a" -> "â"), holding it activates the apps
;;     layer. Because it's a genuine tap/hold key (not a plain hold-modifier
;;     like fn or Hyper), there IS a tap-timeout window before a lone tap
;;     resolves to "^" - but the alias below uses `tap-hold-press` (not
;;     plain `tap-hold`), which resolves to "hold" the instant another key
;;     is pressed while "^" is still down, regardless of timing, so the
;;     apps-layer shortcuts below have no
;;     perceptible delay; only typing a literal "^" alone waits out the
;;     timeout.
;;   - "^" + s/d/o/m go to (or launch) the setup/daily-note/obsidian/mail
;;     dedicated kitty instances (kitty/sessions/*.kitty-session), via
;;     kitty-focus-or-launch.sh (open-daily-note.sh for d,
;;     which wraps it - see those scripts for why plain `open -a kitty
;;     --args --session <file>` doesn't work here).
;;   - F1-F12 are listed in defsrc explicitly and given real media-key
;;     outputs in the default layer (brightness/mission control/spotlight/
;;     dictation/do-not-disturb/media-transport/volume - see the row itself
;;     below), because kanata takes over the entire keyboard: any key NOT
;;     listed in defsrc is passed through as raw literal input, which is
;;     why the F-row stopped acting as media keys once kanata started
;;     managing this keyboard. Explicitly mapping F1-F12 restores stock
;;     macOS behaviour, and there are now three ways to reach the row:
;;       bare F-key           media action (brightness, volume, ...)
;;       Shift + F-key        the literal F1-F12
;;       "^" + F-key          the literal F1-F12 (apps layer)
;;     Shift is the access modifier, not part of the output: the fk1-fk12
;;     aliases wrap the function key in `unshift`, so an app sees a clean F5
;;     rather than Shift+F5. A genuinely shifted function key is still
;;     available as "^" + Shift + F-key, since the apps layer passes the row
;;     through untouched. Note fn itself is no longer in
;;     defsrc at all, so it's passed straight through unmodified - it may
;;     or may not regain its native macOS behaviour (emoji picker/
;;     dictation/etc.) depending on how kanata's virtual keyboard reports
;;     it; not verified.
;;   - Gotcha: German QWERTZ swaps Y and Z compared to US QWERTY, but
;;     kanata's key names always follow the US/ANSI physical-position
;;     naming. So the physical key printed "Z" on your keyboard is named
;;     "y" in kanata, and the physical key printed "Y" is named "z" - that's
;;     why the Zen shortcut below is bound to "y", not "z".
;;
;; IMPORTANT - this config requires a kanata build with the "cmd" feature,
;; which the plain Homebrew build does NOT have. Build it yourself instead:
;;   brew install rust
;;   cargo install kanata --features cmd
;; This installs to ~/.cargo/bin/kanata. Point your LaunchDaemon's
;; ProgramArguments at that path instead of /opt/homebrew/bin/kanata.
;; The `danger-enable-cmd yes` line below is required for `cmd` actions to
;; run at all - it means this config file can execute arbitrary shell
;; commands, so keep it readable/writable only by yourself (it already is,
;; under your user's home directory).
;;
;; The app names below ("Zen", "kitty", "Bitwarden", "Claude") must exactly
;; match what's in /Applications - check with `ls /Applications` and adjust
;; the (cmd open -a "...") lines below if any differ.
;;
;; Also requires the Karabiner-DriverKit-VirtualHIDDevice driver on macOS,
;; and kanata must be granted Input Monitoring (and ideally Accessibility)
;; permissions in System Settings > Privacy & Security.
;;
;; Run manually to test (also validates syntax without doing anything if you
;; add --check):
;;   sudo kanata --cfg /path/to/kanata.kbd
;;
;; If kanata does not pick up your MacBook's built-in keyboard automatically,
;; check its startup log for the exact device name and uncomment
;; "macos-dev-names-include" below with that name.

(defcfg
  process-unmapped-keys yes
  concurrent-tap-hold yes
  danger-enable-cmd yes
  ;; macos-dev-names-include (
  ;;   "Apple Internal Keyboard / Trackpad"
  ;; )
)

(defvar
  tap-timeout 200
  hold-timeout 200
)

(defsrc
  ;    '    [    caps  nubs y    k    b    c    t    s    d    o    r    m
  f1   f2   f3   f4    f5   f6   f7   f8   f9   f10  f11  f12
)

(defalias
  ;; tap: ö (pass the key through unchanged)
  ;; hold: \ (backslash, sent as a literal unicode character)
  oe (tap-hold $tap-timeout $hold-timeout ; (unicode \))

  ;; tap: ä (pass the key through unchanged)
  ;; hold: | (pipe, sent as a literal unicode character)
  ae (tap-hold $tap-timeout $hold-timeout ' (unicode |))

  ;; tap: ü (pass the key through unchanged)
  ;; hold: ~ (tilde, sent as a literal unicode character)
  ue (tap-hold $tap-timeout $hold-timeout [ (unicode ~))

  ;; Hyper: hold Ctrl+Shift+Alt+Cmd together for as long as caps is held
  hyper (multi lctl lsft lalt lmet)

  ;; Activate the "apps" layer below for as long as the apps-layer trigger is held
  apps_layer (layer-while-held apps)

  ;; tap: ^ (pass the key through unchanged, e.g. for accent composition)
  ;; hold: activate the "apps" layer above
  ;; Physical key: on this keyboard the "^" dead key reports as KEY_102ND
  ;; (kanata name "nubs" - the ISO "extra" key), NOT KEY_GRAVE/"grv" as its
  ;; position might suggest - confirmed via `kanata --debug`. Don't assume
  ;; "grv" for this key on other keyboards without checking the debug log.
  ;; Uses tap-hold-press (not plain tap-hold): plain tap-hold only resolves
  ;; on a timer, so pressing e.g. k while nubs is still down within
  ;; tap-timeout would NOT engage the layer if nubs+k are pressed at normal
  ;; typing speed. tap-hold-press activates the hold-action as soon as
  ;; another key is pressed while nubs is held, regardless of timing - so
  ;; nubs+k is instant, and the timeout only applies to tapping nubs alone
  ;; to type a literal "^".
  caret_apps (tap-hold-press $tap-timeout $hold-timeout nubs @apps_layer)

  ;; F-row: bare key = media action, Shift + key = the literal F-key.
  ;;
  ;; `fork` activates its right-hand action when any of the trigger keys is
  ;; active, so each of these sends its media key normally and the function
  ;; key while shift is held.
  ;;
  ;; Shift is deliberately NOT added to defsrc for this. fork tests kanata's
  ;; own active key outputs rather than raw hardware, and a shift that is
  ;; merely passed through still counts as one - but only because
  ;; `process-unmapped-keys yes` is set in defcfg above. The config reference
  ;; calls out that exact dependency for modifiers used this way, so that
  ;; setting is load-bearing here and not just a convenience.
  ;;
  ;; The `unshift` wrapper is what makes Shift+F5 arrive as a real F5 rather
  ;; than as Shift+F5: it releases lsft/rsft for the duration of the press
  ;; only. It releases nothing else, so Ctrl+Shift+F5 still arrives as
  ;; Ctrl+F5 - shift is the access modifier here, not part of the output.
  ;;
  ;; Genuinely shifted function keys are therefore unreachable this way, and
  ;; are not lost: the apps layer passes the F-row through untouched, so
  ;; "^" + Shift + F5 is still Shift+F5.
  fk1    (fork brdn (unshift f1)     (lsft rsft))
  fk2    (fork brup (unshift f2)     (lsft rsft))
  fk3    (fork mctl (unshift f3)     (lsft rsft))
  fk4    (fork sls  (unshift f4)     (lsft rsft))
  fk5    (fork dtn  (unshift f5)     (lsft rsft))
  fk6    (fork dnd  (unshift f6)     (lsft rsft))
  fk7    (fork prev (unshift f7)     (lsft rsft))
  fk8    (fork pp   (unshift f8)     (lsft rsft))
  fk9    (fork next (unshift f9)     (lsft rsft))
  fk10   (fork mute (unshift f10)    (lsft rsft))
  fk11   (fork vold (unshift f11)    (lsft rsft))
  fk12   (fork volu (unshift f12)    (lsft rsft))

  ;; App launch/switch shortcuts, active only while the apps layer is held

  hyper-z    (cmd open -a "Zen")
  hyper-k    (cmd open -a "kitty")
  hyper-b    (cmd open -a "Bitwarden")
  hyper-c    (cmd open -a "Claude")
  hyper-t    (cmd open -a "Microsoft Teams")

  ;; Kitty session shortcuts, active only while the apps layer is held.
  ;; These call kitty-focus-or-launch.sh (see that script for why) rather
  ;; than a bare `open -a kitty --args --session ...`: kitty is a singleton
  ;; app on macOS, and plain `open -a` does NOT forward new --args to an
  ;; already-running instance (it just refocuses whatever was already
  ;; showing - confirmed empirically).
  ;;
  ;; kanata does not expand ~ or $HOME in `cmd` paths, so these go through
  ;; /bin/sh, which does. That keeps this file free of a hardcoded home
  ;; directory - and therefore portable and publishable - without turning it
  ;; into a generated file. `exec` avoids leaving an extra shell around.
  hyper-s    (cmd /bin/sh -c "exec $HOME/.config/kitty/scripts/kitty-focus-or-launch.sh $HOME/.config/kitty/sessions/setup.kitty-session")
  hyper-d    (cmd /bin/sh -c "exec $HOME/.config/kitty/scripts/open-daily-note.sh")
  hyper-o    (cmd /bin/sh -c "exec $HOME/.config/kitty/scripts/kitty-focus-or-launch.sh $HOME/.config/kitty/sessions/obsidian.kitty-session")
  ;; Optional work session. kanata rejects a duplicate alias, so an overlay
  ;; cannot redefine this one - instead the binding lives here permanently and
  ;; simply does nothing until an overlay provides the session file it points
  ;; at. Every alias a layer references has to exist, hence no conditional.
  work-session (cmd /bin/sh -c "s=$HOME/.config/kitty/sessions/work-network.kitty-session; [ -f $s ] && exec $HOME/.config/kitty/scripts/kitty-focus-or-launch.sh $s")
  hyper-m    (cmd /bin/sh -c "exec $HOME/.config/kitty/scripts/kitty-focus-or-launch.sh $HOME/.config/kitty/sessions/mail.kitty-session")
)

(deflayer default
  @oe  @ae  @ue  @hyper  @caret_apps  _  _  _  _  _  _  _  _  _  _
  @fk1 @fk2 @fk3 @fk4 @fk5 @fk6 @fk7 @fk8 @fk9 @fk10 @fk11 @fk12
)

(deflayer apps
  _    _    _    _       _            @hyper-z  @hyper-k  @hyper-b  @hyper-c  @hyper-t  @hyper-s  @hyper-d  @hyper-o  @work-session  @hyper-m
  f1   f2   f3   f4   f5   f6   f7   f8   f9   f10  f11  f12
)
